# Supplementary Materials text. Tables are built from JSON in build script.
MM = [
('h', "Soil carbon system and definitions"),
('p', "We distinguish four soil carbon quantities. Soil organic carbon (SOC) is the organic carbon of the fine earth (≤2 mm), measured by dry combustion or wet oxidation and harmonized in WoSIS [@batjes20]. Soil inorganic carbon (SIC) refers to solid carbonates and, following convention [@huang24], is computed as 12% of the calcium carbonate equivalent (SIC = 0.12 × TCEQ), which includes non-calcium carbonates. Dissolved inorganic carbon and soil CO₂ are not included in stocks. Mineral-associated organic carbon (MAOC) is organic carbon in the fine (<53 µm) or heavy fraction, and particulate organic carbon (POC) is organic carbon in the coarse (>53 µm) or light fraction [@lavallee20;@georgiou22]. Total soil carbon (TC) is SOC + SIC. Carbonate persistence is governed by the carbonate equilibria CO₂(g) + H₂O ↔ H₂CO₃ ↔ HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ and CaCO₃(s) + H⁺ ↔ Ca²⁺ + HCO₃⁻ (eqs. S1 and S2 of [@huang24]); acidification shifts both toward dissolution, and the products are either exported as bicarbonate or degassed as CO₂ [@hamilton07;@west05]."),
('h', "Observation data and quality control"),
('p', "Profile data were taken from the latest WoSIS extract [@batjes20] for pH in water (phaq), total carbonate equivalent (tceq), organic carbon (orgc), fine-earth bulk density by oven-drying (bdfiod), and volumetric coarse fragments (cfvo). Following the screening of Huang *et al.* [@huang24], we (i) removed litter and organic surface layers (flagged as organic surface in WoSIS or designated O, L, F, or H horizons, litter, or forest floor); (ii) removed depth-inconsistent horizons (upper depth ≥ lower depth, negative depths, or upper depth ≥ 200 cm); (iii) removed samples dated before 1970, so that the observation period approximates the present-day climate baseline (undated samples were retained); (iv) applied value-range filters (pH 3–11; TCEQ 0–1000 g kg⁻¹; ORGC 0–580 g kg⁻¹; BD 0.05–2.5 g cm⁻³; CFVO 0–100%); and (v) removed duplicate profile–depth records. Counts after each step are given in table S1. The pre-1970 filter removed the largest number of layers (for example, 78,513 pH layers and 27,779 carbonate layers). After screening, 174,092 of 226,821 profiles contained at least one usable variable. Unlike Huang *et al.*, we did not supplement WoSIS with national resurveys or literature extractions; our SIC training set (99,096 topsoil profiles) is larger in profile count than theirs (55,077 profiles) but is restricted to WoSIS and has fewer measurements per profile at depth."),
('h', "Depth harmonization"),
('p', "Horizon data were harmonized to 0–30, 30–100, and 100–200 cm with mass-preserving equal-area quadratic splines [@bishop99;@malone09] (λ = 0.1). A profile contributed to a target layer only if its deepest observed horizon reached at least the midpoint of that layer; we did not extrapolate beyond observed depths. Single-horizon profiles were assigned their horizon value for the overlapping layer when coverage allowed. Training sample sizes per layer and target are given in table S3."),
('h', "Environmental covariates and prediction grids"),
('p', "We used 33 covariates selected for their known roles in soil formation and carbon dynamics (table S2): the 19 WorldClim 2.1 bioclimatic variables [@fick17]; Hargreaves potential evapotranspiration [@hargreaves85] computed from WorldClim monthly temperature, and the humidity index ln(1 + P/PET); elevation, slope, and topographic position index (TPI, elevation minus the 5 × 5-cell mean); SoilGrids clay, sand, silt (100 − clay − sand), and cation exchange capacity (CEC) [@poggio21], depth-weighted to the target layers; and GLiM lithology [@hartmann12] as a class code plus indicators for carbonate sedimentary rocks, evaporites, and unconsolidated sediments. For SIC models, predicted pH was added as a covariate, following the pH pathway of Huang *et al.* [@huang24]. All covariates were assembled on a 10′ (~18 km) grid, which is the grid on which models were trained, evaluated, and integrated. The land mask excludes Antarctica, land south of 60°S, and the Köppen–Geiger ice-cap class [@beck23]."),
('p', "Thirty-arc-second (~1 km) maps were produced by applying the 10′-trained models to a hybrid covariate stack in which climate and soil covariates were bilinearly interpolated from the 10′ grid and elevation was taken at native 30″ resolution from WorldClim. Streaming the native 30″ bioclimatic archive for all land tiles was input/output-limited on our computing system, and we report this compromise explicitly. Complete global 30″ mosaics exist for pH, SOC, and SIC stocks in all three layers. The 0–30 cm integrals (SOC 908 Pg; SIC 104 Pg) are reported alongside the 10′ integrals (858 and 185 Pg). Deep-layer 30″ SOC integrals (1046 and 2126 Pg for 30–100 and 100–200 cm) exceed the 10′ values because the organic-horizon probability was not nested within the 0–30 cm mosaic at 30″; they are provisional and are not used for any total."),
('h', "Statistical models"),
('p', "All models are gradient-boosted trees fitted with XGBoost (histogram method) [@chen16]. For each depth layer we fitted four model families. (i) pH: regression of pH on covariates. (ii) SIC: a two-step (hurdle) model analogous to that of Huang *et al.* [@huang24], comprising a binary classifier for carbonate presence (SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹) and a regression of ln(SIC) on covariates plus predicted pH for carbonate-bearing samples. Predictions were back-transformed with Duan’s smearing estimator [@duan83] (smearing factors 2.13, 1.73, and 1.57 for the three layers), and the expected SIC content was computed as P(presence) × E[SIC | presence] (eq. S1). (iii) SOC: an organic-horizon classifier (SOC > 120 g kg⁻¹) and a regression of ln(1 + SOC) for mineral soils. The organic-horizon probability was rescaled so that the area of predicted organic soils matched the PEATMAP peatland area of 4.23 Mkm² [@xu18] (scaling factor 0.93), and the organic probability in deeper layers was capped by that of the layer above. Organic-horizon carbon density was computed as the mean of the observed SOC × BD products (116.5, 100.6, and 92.8 kg C m⁻³ for the three layers) rather than as the product of means. (iv) Bulk density: regression of BD on covariates plus ln(1 + SOC)."),
('p', "Each observation was weighted by 1/n_cell, where n_cell is the number of profiles in its 0.5° cell. This declustering weight reduces the influence of dense national survey networks (for example, in the United States and parts of Europe) on the fitted climate relations and reduced the global topsoil SOC estimate from about 960 Pg in an unweighted preliminary model to 858 Pg."),
('h', "Hyperparameter selection and cross-validation"),
('p', "As in Huang *et al.* [@huang24], we used random 10-fold cross-validation as the primary accuracy protocol. For each target and layer, three configurations were drawn at random from a hyperparameter grid (number of trees, 250 or 400; maximum depth, 5, 7, or 9 for regressions and 5 or 7 for classifiers; learning rate, 0.05 or 0.08 for regressions and 0.05 or 0.1 for classifiers; row subsampling, 0.8; column subsampling, 0.7 to 0.9; minimum child weight, 1 or 5) and evaluated by random 10-fold cross-validation, and the configuration with the highest R² (regression) or AUC (classification) was retained (table S4). This search is smaller than that of Huang *et al.*, who also compared gradient boosting and random forests [@huang24]. Classifiers are reported with AUC, F1 score, precision, and recall; regressions are reported with R² and RMSE on the original scale. For SIC, the combined hurdle R² compares out-of-fold expected SIC with observations among all samples, and the log-scale R² of the amount model is reported separately (table S3). Random cross-validation estimates interpolation skill; under spatial autocorrelation it is optimistic for prediction into sparsely sampled regions [@ploton20;@meyer21]. We did not use spatial cross-validation to rank models or to compare with Huang *et al.*, because their published metrics are random-fold metrics."),
('h', "Stock computation"),
('p', "For each 10′ cell and layer, carbon stocks (Mg C ha⁻¹) were computed as C = c × BD × (1 − CF/100) × h × 0.1 (eq. S2), where c is the concentration (g kg⁻¹), BD the fine-earth bulk density (g cm⁻³), CF the volumetric coarse-fragment content (%), and h the layer thickness (cm). SOC stocks combine the mineral and organic components weighted by the organic-horizon probability; SIC is assigned only to the mineral fraction. Global totals were obtained by multiplying stocks by cell area. National and continental totals use the same country boundary raster for all figures."),
('h', "Uncertainty"),
('p', "For present-day stocks, uncertainty combines the spread across 10 random-10-fold fold-models of the final workflow with Monte Carlo perturbations of bulk density [systematic factor N(1, 0.05)], coarse fragments [multiplicative log-uniform (0.5, 2) of the layer median, capped at 60%], effective thickness [N(1, 0.05) for 30–100 cm and N(1, 0.10) for 100–200 cm], and organic C density [N(1, 0.20)]. We report the mean, 90% CI (5th to 95th percentiles), and ±1 SD (Huang style; fig. S14 and table S5). Perturbation ranges are labeled assumptions. For climate projections, the same 10 fold-models are crossed with six GCMs (n = 60 for equilibrium); time-limited responses additionally sample τ log-uniformly within literature ranges. Headline values are ensemble means with 90% CIs; maps are ensemble means so that map-area integrals equal headline means."),
('h', "Future climate scenarios and equilibrium change"),
('p', "Future climate covariates for 2081–2100 were taken from the WorldClim CMIP6 downscaled monthly and bioclimatic fields at 10′, which are calibrated against the WorldClim 2.1 baseline (1970–2000), for six GCMs (ACCESS-CM2, EC-Earth3-Veg, MPI-ESM1-2-HR, UKESM1-0-LL, IPSL-CM6A-LR, and MIROC6) under SSP1-2.6, SSP2-4.5, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5 [@oneill16]. PET and the humidity index were recomputed from future monthly temperature and precipitation. For 30″ products, the same anomalies were added to the present-day 30″ fields (delta method). Soil, terrain, and lithology covariates were held fixed. Equilibrium change is the difference between predictions under future and present covariates for pH, SIC, SOC, and BD (space-for-time substitution); SIC responds to climate both directly and through predicted pH."),
('p', "A single accounting is used for future change (supplementary text S2). All maps and headlines come from the same ensemble of 10 random-10-fold fold-models of the final workflow × 6 GCMs. Maps are ensemble means, checked so that map-area integrals equal headline means (for example, SSP5-8.5: −378 Pg SOC and +229 Pg SIC). Medians and 90% CIs are also reported. Time-limited responses sample τ log-uniformly within literature ranges."),
('h', "Time-limited response"),
('p', "To estimate the part of the equilibrium change that can be realized by 2100, we assumed a first-order approach to the new equilibrium, Δ(t) = Δ_eq[1 − exp(−t/τ)] (eq. S3), with t = 85 years. τ was sampled log-uniformly per layer from 20–100 years (SOC, 0–30 cm), 100–1000 years (SOC, 30–100 cm), 500–3000 years (SOC, 100–200 cm), and 1000–10,000 years (SIC, all layers), on the basis of global turnover-time and radiocarbon syntheses [@carvalhais14;@he16;@shi20] and the slow kinetics of pedogenic carbonate accumulation [@zamanian16;@monger15]. For MAOC and POC, central τ values of 150 and 15 years were used, with fast (50 and 5 years) and slow (400 and 30 years) alternatives [@lavallee20;@cotrufo19], and t = 80 years (2020–2100). This is a hybrid of digital soil mapping and first-order kinetics, not a land-surface model; it ignores transient overshoot, changes in inputs along the trajectory, and pathway dependence."),
('h', "Climate-zone shifts and trade-off classification"),
('p', "Köppen–Geiger classes for 1991–2020 and 2071–2099 were taken from Beck *et al.* [@beck23] for each SSP. A cell was classified as shifted if its main class changed. Using the ensemble-mean fields for 2081–2100, each cell was assigned to one of four quadrants according to the signs of ΔSOC and ΔSIC (0–200 cm); “trade-off” denotes ΔSOC < 0 and ΔSIC > 0. Under SSP5-8.5 the trade-off area is 69 Mkm². Areas and carbon changes on shifted land are given in table S8."),
('h', "Nitrogen-driven acidification"),
('p', "We followed Huang *et al.* [@huang24] in using the empirical relation between cumulative chemical nitrogen fertilizer input over 30 years (N_in, Mg N ha⁻¹) and the change in topsoil pH derived from two national soil surveys in China, ΔpH = −0.06 N_in + 0.11 (eq. S4) [@guo10]. Huang *et al.* drove this relation with GLOBIOM projections for three INMS nitrogen-policy scenarios. GLOBIOM/INMS gridded fertilizer fields were not obtained (INMS registration wall; no open Zenodo mirror). We used instead the ISIMIP3b IMAGE fertilizer application rates for five crop types and the corresponding crop fractions [@stehfest14;@lange19] for SSP1-2.6, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5; SSP2-4.5 N_in was interpolated as the mean of SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0 IMAGE inputs. N_in was computed as the cropland-intensity cumulative input over 2020–2050, and ΔpH was applied only to cropland, weighted by cropland fraction. ΔSIC was obtained by finite difference of our SIC model: SIC(pH + ΔpH) − SIC(pH), with all other covariates fixed, applied to each layer (eq. S5). Because the positive intercept implies alkalinization for N_in < 1.83 Mg N ha⁻¹, we report loss-only totals (cells with ΔSIC > 0 set to zero), which are comparable with published loss estimates, together with net totals (table S9). Topsoil loss-only totals are 1.6, 1.8, 2.1, and 2.2 Pg under the four SSPs."),
('p', "We ran three sensitivity analyses. (i) Fertilizer plus atmospheric deposition (NHx + NOy from ISIMIP3b). (ii) A stoichiometric alternative in which carbonate carbon loss equals 0.21 kg C per kg N applied (range 0.055 to 0.32) [@zamanian18]. (iii) A high-N sensitivity, in which cropland N_in was multiplied by the scaling factor that reproduces each of the topsoil losses of Huang *et al.* (10, 14, and 23 Pg) under the same ΔpH relation and our SIC response. The scaling factor is diagnostic: it quantifies how much larger the nitrogen trajectory must be, not a plausible scenario."),
('h', "Mineral-associated and particulate organic carbon"),
('p', "Fraction data were taken from the open synthesis of Georgiou *et al.* [@georgiou22;@georgiou_data], which compiles particle-size (<53 µm versus >53 µm) and density fractionation data from published studies, ISRAD, and a LUCAS subset (1457 records). We computed f_MAOC = MAOC/(MAOC + POC) and removed records without coordinates or depth, records with f_MAOC outside (0, 1], records in which POC was not measured (POC = 0 or f_MAOC ≥ 0.995), records with mass-balance recovery outside 0.7 to 1.3, and records with depth midpoints below 100 cm (table S12). A total of 1005 records with complete covariates were retained (particle size, 874; density, 122; combined, 9; topsoil, 980). Other compilations largely overlap with ISRAD and LUCAS and were not added to avoid duplication."),
('p', "We fitted XGBoost regressions of logit(f_MAOC) on the covariate stack plus depth midpoint and fractionation method, with 0.5° declustering weights, and compared five hyperparameter configurations by random 10-fold cross-validation (table S4). The best configuration gave R² = 0.58 (five repeats of 10-fold cross-validation, 0.57 ± 0.02), RMSE = 0.129, and bias = +0.016; R² for MAOC concentration was 0.71. An initial fit that retained records without measured POC gave R² = 0.63, but this value was inflated by records with f_MAOC = 1; we rejected that model. Maps were predicted for the particle-size definition. MAOC stocks were computed as mineral-soil SOC × f_MAOC and POC as the remainder; organic horizons are reported separately. Fifty bootstrap refits provided 90% CIs. Future f_MAOC and mineral SOC were re-predicted under each GCM and SSP with the final model generation; ΔMAOC + ΔPOC is therefore not directly additive with the ΔSOC of the projection ensemble."),
('h', "Driver attribution"),
('p', "Exact TreeSHAP contributions [@lundberg17;@lundberg20] were computed for 30,000 randomly selected land cells for the topsoil pH, SIC presence, SIC amount, mineral SOC, and f_MAOC models. Covariates were grouped into climate (bioclimatic variables, PET, humidity index), soil (texture, CEC, predicted pH), topography (elevation, slope, TPI), parent material (GLiM classes), and sampling (depth and fractionation method; f_MAOC only). Group importance is the sum of mean |SHAP| values within a group as a percentage of the total (table S11). Dominant-driver maps assign each cell the group with the largest summed |SHAP|, computed with approximate SHAP for all cells. Partial dependence was computed over the observed covariate range, and breakpoints were located by fitting two-segment piecewise-linear functions. The observed pH–SIC relation was obtained by fitting a logistic function, P(SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹) = 1/{1 + exp[−k(pH − pH₅₀)]}, to binned WoSIS topsoil observations (n = 91,273), giving pH₅₀ = 7.10 and k = 3.41. A standardized linear path model (n = 58,404 topsoil observations with pH, SIC, and SOC) estimated the direct and indirect effects of the humidity index, mean annual temperature, clay, and carbonate rock on pH, SIC, and SOC; indirect effects are products of path coefficients."),
('h', "Independent validation"),
('p', "Predicted topsoil SOC stocks were compared with 15,378 LUCAS 2018 topsoil samples [@orgiazzi18], which were not used for training the SOC models. LUCAS 2018 topsoil stocks are 0–20 cm. Comparing them directly to the 0–30 cm map inflated mean bias to +28.6 Mg C ha⁻¹ (RMSE = 58.6; R² = −0.46). After depth harmonization (scaling the prediction by a Jobbágy–Jackson depth profile factor so that 0–20/0–30 ≈ 0.74), mean bias falls to +6.9 Mg C ha⁻¹ (RMSE = 48.0; R² ≈ 0.02). This is a validation fix, not a map recalibration; we report the residual positive bias and do not apply a multiplicative LUCAS correction to the global maps. An IMAGE cropland-fraction covariate was tested and rejected (random-CV R² change +0.001; global mean concentration +0.7%)."),
]

ST = [
('h', "S1. Comparison with previous estimates"),
('p', "Table S6 compares our estimates with published global inventories. Earlier SIC estimates were obtained by multiplying mean carbonate contents by the area of soil units [@schlesinger82;@batjes96;@lal99;@plaza18]; Huang *et al.* used digital soil mapping with about 60 covariates and 223,593 measurements [@huang24]. Our 0–1 m SIC (780 Pg) lies within the range of soil-unit estimates (695 to 940 Pg), and our 0–2 m SIC [1598 Pg (1408 to 1801; ±119)] is close to the WISE30sec-based 1558 Pg [@plaza18] and about 30% below the 2305 ± 636 Pg of Huang *et al.*; the new CI upper bound (1801) overlaps the lower half of Huang’s ±1 SD. Several factors probably contribute: Huang *et al.* used additional national and literature data (notably from China), 1-km covariates including vegetation, drought, salinity, and anthropogenic predictors, and a larger SoilGrids-based soil extent; our SIC amount model has lower skill and smearing-based back-transformation may under-represent extreme values. Our SOC estimates agree with Batjes [@batjes96] at 0–2 m [2506 (2263 to 2761; ±151) versus 2376 to 2456 Pg]. Topsoil SOC [848 (774 to 924; ±46)] remains higher than Batjes and SoilGrids; after depth harmonization, LUCAS mean bias is +6.9 Mg C ha⁻¹ (not a global rescale). Our 0–1 m MAOC [1048 (946 to 1152; ±62)] lies between two accountings of Georgiou *et al.* [@georgiou22]."),
('h', "S2. Unified map–headline accounting"),
('p', "Present-day stocks (Figs. 1 and 2; table S5; fig. S14) are means of the final 10′ models with Monte Carlo 90% CIs and ±1 SD. Future changes use one ensemble throughout: 10 random-10-fold fold-models of the final workflow × 6 GCMs. Maps (Figs. 3 and 5) are ensemble means; headline values are the corresponding ensemble means with 90% CIs (5th to 95th percentiles) and ±1 SD. Checked under SSP5-8.5: map integrals equal headline means (−378 Pg SOC; +229 Pg SIC). Time-limited totals additionally sample τ. This replaces the earlier split between a preliminary-model headline ensemble and central-model maps. Values for all SSPs are given in tables S7 and S8."),
('p', "Two further notes. First, the MAOC and POC projections (Fig. 6; table S10) use the mineral-SOC model and the fraction model, exclude organic horizons, and report equilibrium and by-2100 (τ-sampled) changes; under SSP5-8.5, equilibrium ΔMAOC/ΔPOC = −78/−39 Pg and by-2100 = −37/−39 Pg in 0–30 cm. These are not directly additive with total ΔSOC. Second, nitrogen-driven SIC losses in Fig. 4 are loss-only totals; topsoil IMAGE losses are 1.6/1.8/2.1/2.2 Pg (SSP1-2.6/2-4.5/3-7.0/5-8.5), with 0–200 cm losses 13.4/15.1/17.2/17.4 Pg."),
('h', "S3. The pH–SIC relation"),
('p', "Huang *et al.* derived a five-parameter sigmoid relating soil pH to SIC_pH, the Shapley contribution of pH to SIC content, with a global midpoint x₀ = 7.89 and steep changes between pH 7 and 8.5 [@huang24]. Our observed relation describes a different quantity, the probability that carbonate is present, and is centered at pH₅₀ = 7.10 (k = 3.41); the partial dependence of modeled presence on predicted pH shows a breakpoint at 6.64, and the partial dependence of SIC amount has the same breakpoint (fig. S5, C and D). The median SIC content of carbonate-bearing soils rises steeply between pH 7 and 8 and plateaus near pH 8.4 (fig. S6B), consistent with the theoretical pH of a soil solution in equilibrium with calcite (~8.2) noted by Huang *et al.* The two relations are therefore complementary: carbonates begin to occur near neutral pH and accumulate as pH approaches calcite equilibrium. Soils with pH between about 6.6 and 7.5 are those in which carbonate presence is most sensitive to acidification."),
('h', "S4. Why the nitrogen estimates differ"),
('p', "Under IMAGE projections, mean cumulative cropland fertilizer input over 2020–2050 is about 2.6 to 2.7 Mg N ha⁻¹ under SSP1-2.6, SSP3-7.0, and SSP5-8.5 (SSP2-4.5 interpolated), and only about half of cropland cells experience a pH decline because of the positive intercept of eq. S4. Loss-only topsoil totals are 1.6, 1.8, 2.1, and 2.2 Pg under the four SSPs (0–200 cm: 13.4, 15.1, 17.2, and 17.4 Pg). Simple ratios of Huang *et al.*’s 10, 14, and 23 Pg GLOBIOM targets to these IMAGE losses imply roughly 6- to 11-fold larger nitrogen inputs. GLOBIOM/INMS grids were not obtained. The ΔpH relation integrates fertilizer effects under Chinese cropping systems with high N surpluses; its transferability to other regions, soil buffering capacities, and liming regimes is the largest single uncertainty in global estimates."),
('h', "S5. Equilibrium versus time-limited responses"),
('p', "Fig. S13 and table S7 show that both equilibrium and time-limited ΔTC are negative under SSP5-8.5 [eq −150 (−245 to −41); TL −180 (−282 to −100)], and that TL 90% CIs exclude zero under all scenarios. Equilibrium SIC gains only partly offset SOC losses; by 2100, realized SIC change remains small because even the shortest assumed SIC turnover time (1000 years) realizes only a few percent of the equilibrium change in 85 years. For organic fractions under SSP5-8.5, equilibrium ΔMAOC/ΔPOC = −78/−39 Pg and by-2100 = −37/−39 Pg (0–30 cm); POC is nearly fully realized, whereas MAOC carries a larger committed loss (fig. S7). These results are conditional on the space-for-time equilibrium being a reasonable target state and on first-order kinetics."),
('h', "S6. Limitations"),
('p', "(i) Space-for-time substitution assumes that present-day spatial relations between climate and soil carbon apply to future climates; it omits land-use and management change, irrigation, dust deposition of calcium, changes in vegetation and organic inputs, CO₂ fertilization, and direct effects of soil pCO₂ on carbonate equilibria. (ii) τ values are assumptions bracketing published ranges, not fitted parameters. (iii) The covariate set lacks vegetation, drought-index, topographic-wetness, salinity, and nitrogen-deposition predictors. (iv) Random cross-validation is optimistic for prediction into sparsely sampled regions [@ploton20;@meyer21]. (v) After depth harmonization, topsoil SOC retains a residual LUCAS bias of +6.9 Mg C ha⁻¹; we do not rescale global maps. (vi) The 30″ maps use climate interpolated from 10′; 30″ deep-layer SOC integrals are provisional. (vii) Present-day stocks now carry fold-model × Monte Carlo CIs (BD, CF, thickness, organic C-density assumptions labeled). (viii) Future projections are now on the final fold-models; maps and headlines share one ensemble. (ix) Nitrogen analysis still uses IMAGE rather than GLOBIOM (not obtained); SSP2-4.5 is interpolated; the China-calibrated ΔpH relation is extrapolated. (x) Subsoil MAOC rests on 34 fraction records. (xi) We did not quantify SIC fluxes to inland waters or the fate of dissolved carbonate [@huang24;@hamilton07]. (xii) SIC 0–200 cm remains below Huang’s mean, although the CI overlaps the lower half of Huang’s ±1 SD."),
]

FIGS = [
("FigS1_random_CV.png", "Random 10-fold cross-validation.", "(A) Cross-validated skill of the final models by depth layer: R² for pH, AUC for SIC presence, combined hurdle R² for SIC, R² for mineral SOC, and R² for bulk density (values in table S3). (B) Observed versus cross-validated f_MAOC for 1005 fraction records (hexagonal bins; dashed line, 1:1). R² = 0.58 (five repeats of 10-fold cross-validation: 0.57 ± 0.02); RMSE = 0.129."),
("FigS2_fraction_data.png", "Fraction observations.", "(A) Locations of the 1005 harmonized MAOC/POC records by fractionation method (particle size, n = 874; density, n = 122; combined, n = 9), from the synthesis of Georgiou et al. (including ISRAD and LUCAS subsets). (B) Distribution of observed f_MAOC by method (box, interquartile range; line, median; whiskers, 1.5 × interquartile range)."),
("FigS3_SHAP_top_features.png", "Most important predictors.", "Top eight covariates by share of mean |SHAP| (%) for (A) pH, (B) SIC presence, (C) SIC amount, (D) mineral SOC, and (E) f_MAOC (topsoil models; 30,000 random land cells). logAI, ln(1 + P/PET); bio1 to bio19, WorldClim bioclimatic variables; pH_pred, predicted pH; depth_mid, sample depth midpoint."),
("FigS4_SHAP_dependence.png", "SHAP dependence.", "SHAP values of selected covariates: (A) SIC presence versus ln(1 + P/PET); (B) SIC presence versus predicted pH; (C) SIC amount versus predicted pH; (D) pH versus ln(1 + P/PET); (E) mineral SOC versus mean annual temperature (bio1, °C); (F) mineral SOC versus ln(1 + P/PET); (G) f_MAOC versus clay (%); (H) f_MAOC versus mean annual temperature. Each point is one land cell."),
("FigS5_PDP_thresholds.png", "Partial dependence and breakpoints.", "Partial dependence (red) and breakpoints of two-segment piecewise-linear fits (dashed; values in table S11) for (A to C) SIC presence versus ln(1 + P/PET), mean annual precipitation (mm), and predicted pH; (D) SIC amount (g kg⁻¹) versus predicted pH; (E to I) f_MAOC versus clay, silt, CEC, mean annual temperature, and ln(1 + P/PET); (J and K) mineral SOC (g kg⁻¹) versus mean annual temperature and ln(1 + P/PET); and (L) pH versus ln(1 + P/PET)."),
("FigS6_pH_SIC_curve.png", "Observed pH–SIC relation.", "(A) Probability of carbonate presence (SIC > 0.1 g kg⁻¹) versus pH in WoSIS topsoil observations (purple points, binned means; n = 91,273), fitted logistic function (black; pH₅₀ = 7.10, k = 3.41), and partial dependence of the SIC presence model (red). (B) Median SIC content of carbonate-bearing samples by pH bin (log scale). Compare the sigmoid of Huang et al., which describes the Shapley contribution of pH to SIC amount (supplementary text S3)."),
("FigS7_tau_sensitivity.png", "Sensitivity of MAOC and POC changes to turnover time.", "Change in topsoil (0–30 cm) MAOC (A) and POC (B) by SSP: equilibrium (darkest) and time-limited by 2100 for three turnover times (MAOC, τ = 50, 150, and 400 years; POC, τ = 5, 15, and 30 years). Values in table S10."),
("FigS8_path_analysis.png", "Standardized path model.", "Standardized path coefficients linking the humidity index [ln(P/PET)], mean annual temperature (MAT), clay, and carbonate rock to topsoil pH, SIC, and SOC (n = 58,404). Blue, positive; red, negative; arrow width proportional to |β|. Indirect effect of the humidity index on SIC through pH, −0.38; total effect, −0.35. R² = 0.34 (pH), 0.44 (SIC), and 0.29 (SOC). The linear model is descriptive and complements the nonlinear SHAP analysis."),
("FigS9_deep_layers.png", "Subsoil stocks.", "SOC (A and C) and SIC (B and D) stocks (Mg C ha⁻¹) in 30–100 cm (A and B) and 100–200 cm (C and D) on the 10′ grid; insets give the proportion of land area per class. Global totals: SOC 818 and 841 Pg; SIC 602 and 819 Pg."),
("FigS10_MAOC_uncertainty_subsoil.png", "MAOC uncertainty and subsoil fractions.", "(A) Standard deviation of topsoil f_MAOC (percentage points) across 50 bootstrap refits. (B and C) MAOC (B) and POC (C) stocks in 30–100 cm (Mg C ha⁻¹); insets give continental shares. Subsoil totals: MAOC 538 Pg (90% CI, 516 to 557), POC 189 Pg (169 to 210); based on only 34 subsoil fraction records."),
("FigS11_dominant_driver_pH_SIC.png", "Dominant drivers of pH and SIC amount.", "Covariate group with the largest summed |SHAP| per cell for topsoil pH (A) and SIC amount (B); insets give the proportion of land area per group (climate, soil, topography, parent material)."),
("FigS12_30arcsec_zoom.png", "Thirty-arc-second detail.", "Topsoil (0–30 cm) MAOC (A and B) and SIC (C and D) stocks (Mg C ha⁻¹) at 30 arc sec (~1 km) for the North China Plain and Loess Plateau (A and C) and western Europe (B and D). MAOC at 30″ was computed by applying the 10′ MAOC share of SOC to 30″ SOC (global 0–30 cm MAOC integral, 610 Pg). Grey, outside the land mask or no data. Note that color scales differ between panels."),
("FigS13_equilibrium_vs_time_limited.png", "Equilibrium versus time-limited changes in total soil carbon.", "Change in SOC, SIC, and total carbon (TC) in 0–200 cm by 2081–2100 under four SSPs. Dark bars, equilibrium (Eq.); light bars, time-limited by 2100 (TL). Bars show ensemble means and error bars show 90% CIs of the 10 fold-models × 6 GCMs ensemble (time-limited values include τ uncertainty). Under SSP5-8.5, eq ΔTC = −150 (−245 to −41) and TL ΔTC = −180 (−282 to −100). Values in table S7."),
("FigS14_present_uncertainty.png", "Present-day stock uncertainty.", "Uncertainty of present-day global stocks from 10 fold-models × Monte Carlo perturbations of bulk density, coarse fragments, effective thickness, and organic C density. Bars show means; error bars show 90% CIs; ±1 SD is reported in table S5 (Huang style). SOC 0–200: 2506 (2263–2761; ±151); SIC 0–200: 1598 (1408–1801; ±119); TC 0–200: 4105 (3675–4549; ±265)."),
]
